From 0aa2d31f411aabee8e875a4504f0636051657238 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 00:02:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: an error event appended to a relayed stream begins
 after a blank line
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The relay in passthrough writes the upstream's bytes as they come, and
ends a stream cut short with the protocol's own error event (936a6fc7,
truncated inside the data line — left the client mid-line, and the
appended "event: error" landed inside that line: the client read one
event whose data was the truncated half and the error joined, which no
SDK parses, so the error agents act on (#370's point) never arrived as
one. The same append in holdWriter.cutLoop joined onto the reply's last
write the same way, where the encoder's leading message_start landed in
the line still pending.

What is appended now begins after a blank line: one where the client is
at a line's end, two where it is mid-line, so the event still pending
stands and the failure is read as an event of its own. Whole streams
are untouched.

What changed:
- passthrough tracks the line its last write ended on and prefixes the
  failure event it appends accordingly (internal/gateway/gateway.go).
- holdWriter.cutLoop asks its own atLine before streamError
  (internal/gateway/fallback.go).
- TestPassthroughStreamCutMidLine, TestPassthroughStreamEndsOnLineEnd
  and TestLoopCutMidLine fail without the change; the family's
  TestPassthroughStream* and TestTranslate* stay green.

Run: go test -tags nogui -p 2 ./internal/gateway/ (full package, 299s,
ok); gofmt -l internal/gateway/ clean; go vet ./... and GOOS=linux
go vet -tags nogui ./... and GOOS=windows go vet ./... clean; go build
and GOOS=linux go build -tags nogui and GOOS=windows go build clean
(known warnings: trayimage_darwin.go cgo -Wenum-conversion, duplicate
ld libraries). Measured against the real binary in a sandbox: an
Anthropic upstream that dies mid-line reaches a curl client as
"stop_re" + "event: error" on one line before the change, and as
"stop_re", a blank line, then the error event after it.

